  (:adr/body "# ADR 2799007549: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 7549 Craft and Related Workers Not Elsewhere Classified workshop scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 7549\n-- Craft and Related Workers Not Elsewhere Classified** only, Wave3\n(production/trades) of the rollout, and completes the entire ISCO-08\n75xx craft-trades range (7511-7536 all previously landed across prior\nbatches; 7549 is the residual NEC entry that closes the range out).\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"7549\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"7549\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Craft and Related Workers Not\nElsewhere Classified\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to\nthis ADR the entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with\n`:required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no\n`:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7549` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before\nscaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry edit promote the\n`7549` entry to `:implemented`.\n\nISCO-08 7549 is a broad, generic residual \"Not Elsewhere Classified\"\ncategory covering diverse craft work not captured by any more specific\nISCO craft code in the 75xx range (butchers/bakers/dairy/food\npreservation 7511-7516; wood treaters/cabinet makers 7521-7522;\ntailoring/sewing/upholstery/tanning/shoemaking 7531-7536). Standard\nworkshop safety hazards apply generically (hand-tool injury,\nvaried-material handling) without a single dominant hazard type, in\ncontrast to the more technique-specific hazard profiles of its\nsibling 75xx entries -- so this actor's hazard-escalation vocabulary\nis deliberately generic (hand-tool-hazard / material-handling-hazard /\nequipment-condition) rather than technique-specific, matching the\nsame structural pattern already used for the sibling residual category\nISCO-08 7319 (Handicraft Workers Not Elsewhere Classified, 731x\nhandicraft range) rather than any of the more specific 75xx entries.\nThe controlling constraint for this vertical is that the closed op\nallowlist must NEVER include any op that directly finalizes a\ncraft-execution decision (e.g. deciding a craft item is finished) or a\nproduct-quality/safety-clearance decision (e.g. declaring an item\nquality- or safety-cleared), nor override a shop safety officer's\njudgment -- those decisions must always be a hard permanent block,\nnever auto-commit-eligible. This actor is deliberately scoped to\nWORKSHOP SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS COORDINATION ONLY -- it never performs\ncraft work or makes quality/safety-clearance decisions itself.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored closely for module SHAPE and\nthe closed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety\npattern is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7319`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7319)\n(Handicraft Workers Not Elsewhere Classified: `store`/`advisor`/\n`governor`/`actor` split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set,\nalready landed with the near-identical generic-residual-craft-category\nsafety pattern -- the closest possible structural match, being the\nsibling residual NEC category for the adjacent 731x handicraft range)\n-- cloned and read in full in this session. Its `deps.edn` `langgraph`\ngit-sha pin was reused verbatim for this repo (confirmed identical to\nthe most recently landed 75xx sibling, `cloud-itonami-isco-7535`). The\ncrew-member/worker provenance field was renamed from 7319's\n`:artisan-id` to `:worker-id` throughout (store, advisor, governor,\nactor, tests) to match this task's own domain-design wording (\"worker/\nworkshop record must be independently verified/registered before any\naction\"), since ISCO-08 7549's title (\"Craft and Related Workers\", not\n\"Handicraft Workers\") is broader than the artisan/handicraft framing\nof 7319.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7549` as an OSS workshop\nscheduling/logistics-coordination actor for generic craft workshops,\nfollowing the SAME governed-actor architecture as every prior\n`cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Craft Trades Coordination\nAdvisor ⊣ CraftNecGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph\n(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the\nreference's `deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the\n`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family),\nwith an independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via\n`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **workshop scheduling/logistics\ncoordination ONLY, never direct craft-execution or\nquality/safety-clearance authority** -- it must NEVER be designed to\nexercise, simulate exercising, or propose exercising a\ncraft-execution decision (e.g. deciding a craft item is finished), a\nproduct-quality/safety-clearance decision (e.g. declaring an item\nquality- or safety-cleared), or any override of a shop safety\nofficer's judgment. These are not merely gated behind escalation --\nthey are structurally absent from the op-allowlist entirely.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- job/commission/progress data logging\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/task scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a hand-tool-hazard/\n  material-handling/equipment-condition concern, ALWAYS escalates,\n  NEVER auto-commit eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- craft-materials procurement proposal\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles** a\ncraft-execution decision, a product-quality/safety-clearance decision,\nor a shop-safety-officer-judgment override. These are **structurally\nabsent from the op-allowlist entirely**, not merely gated behind\nescalation -- an op such as `:finalize-craft-item` cannot even be\nproposed by the reference mock advisor (its `infer`/`rationale-for`\nfunctions only ever emit one of the four allowlist ops), and if any\ncode path (a forged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving\nfork) ever produced such an op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD\ncheck (`:unknown-op`) rejects it unconditionally and permanently. A\nsecond, independent HARD check (`:scope-excluded-action`) names six\nconcretely-forbidden ops (`craftnec.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:\n`:finalize-craft-item`, `:finalize-craft-execution-decision`,\n`:declare-product-quality-cleared`, `:declare-safety-clearance`,\n`:override-shop-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`:override-safety-officer-judgment`) as defense-in-depth, and is\nverified by dedicated unit tests\n(`craftnec.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-craft-item`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-craft-execution-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-declare-product-quality-cleared`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-declare-safety-clearance`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-shop-safety-officer-judgment`),\nplus a full-graph-run regression\n(`craftnec.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-op-even-at-high-confidence`,\n`.../holds-a-quality-safety-clearance-op-even-at-high-confidence`).\n\n### Four HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Worker unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered crew-member record (`:no-worker`). Never\n   trusts the proposal's own claim of who the worker is.\n2. **Workshop unregistered** -- any proposal must cite an\n   independently registered workshop (`:no-workshop`). Together with\n   check 1, this directly implements the CRITICAL invariant that a\n   worker/workshop record must be independently verified/registered\n   before any action.\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance)\n   (`:no-actuation`).\n4. **Op not allowed / scope-excluded action** -- any `:op` outside the\n   closed four-op allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`),\n   and independently, any proposal whose `:op` names one of the six\n   concretely-forbidden ops above, OR whose free-text `:rationale`\n   names a finalization/execution action for finalizing a craft item,\n   declaring a product quality- or safety-clearance, or overriding a\n   shop safety officer's judgment, is a second HARD, PERMANENT block\n   (`:scope-excluded-action`), evaluated unconditionally on content --\n   defense-in-depth on top of the allowlist check: even if a future\n   change somehow widened the allowlist, content-based rejection still\n   independently blocks craft-execution/quality-clearance/\n   safety-override language wherever it appears in free text.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's implicit always-risky check). This is the\n  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface a hand-tool-hazard/\n  material-handling/equipment-condition observation -- it never\n  resolves the concern itself, and the robot's role ends at \"here is\n  the hazard data,\" never \"here is what I recommend doing about shop\n  safety.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not a\n  hard block, per the task's explicit domain design: an over-threshold\n  craft-materials order is not itself unsafe, unlike a\n  craft-execution/quality-clearance/safety-officer-override attempt,\n  which is why it is handled by a different mechanism (soft escalate\n  vs. structural absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"craft\", \"quality\", \"safety\") can accidentally match inside the mock\nadvisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this is exactly the risk\nCLAUDE.md's worked example warns about for this task.\n`craftnec.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is phrased exclusively as\nfinalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g. \"finalize the craft item\",\n\"declare the product quality-cleared\", \"override the shop safety\nofficer's judgment\" -- matching the task's own worked example\nphrasing verbatim), never bare nouns, and\n`craftnec.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-on-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor, never trips\n`:scope-excluded-action`. The mock advisor's own default rationale\ntemplate was deliberately written to contain the bare nouns (\"logged\nwork record for worker ...\", \"scheduled crew operation for craft\ntask ...\", \"... routed for shop safety officer review\") WITHOUT\ncontaining any of the full finalization-action phrases, so the\nregression test is a genuine stress test of the bare-noun-vs-\naction-phrase distinction, not a vacuous pass. Green on first run in\nthis session -- the scope-exclusion self-trip guard itself was never\ntripped during development.\n`craftnec.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-op-even-at-high-confidence`\nand `.../holds-a-quality-safety-clearance-op-even-at-high-confidence`\nadditionally exercise the same invariant through the full graph run,\nconfirming a scope-excluded proposal never reaches `commit-record!`\nregardless of disposition path.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"7549\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7549` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-7549`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately\n  before the edit).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 24 tests, 52 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n- This ADR completes the entire ISCO-08 75xx craft-trades range\n  (7511-7549) -- every unit group in the range now has a landed,\n  `:implemented` actor.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-craft-execution` or\n  `:approve-quality-clearance` that is merely gated behind an\n  always-escalate list: rejected outright per the task's explicit\n  domain-design guidance -- an always-escalate op still means the\n  *capability* to finalize a craft-execution or quality/safety-\n  clearance decision exists in the code path and is merely deferred to\n  a human gate. For a decision this severe (hand-tool injury,\n  varied-material handling, product-quality/safety clearance), the\n  capability itself must not exist. The only op related to shop-safety\n  escalation is `:flag-safety-concern`, which surfaces the need for\n  human/shop-safety-officer attention and never itself decides,\n  authorizes, or defers a craft-execution or quality/safety-clearance\n  decision for later automatic execution.\n- Making the supply-cost ceiling a HARD block: rejected per the task's\n  explicit domain design -- an over-threshold craft-materials order is\n  not itself unsafe, so it is handled as a soft escalate gate, not a\n  hard block; only the craft-execution/quality-clearance/\n  safety-officer-override class is a hard, unconditional block.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"craft\" \"quality\"\n  \"safety\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip bug\n  class described above (and CLAUDE.md's own worked example for this\n  exact task), and per the explicit instruction to phrase\n  scope-exclusion terms as finalization/execution ACTIONS, never bare\n  nouns -- this actor's own domain necessarily discusses craft/\n  quality/safety-officer concepts to do its scheduling/logging job, so\n  a bare-noun list would risk self-blocking the actor's own legitimate\n  proposals (regression-tested directly against rationale text that\n  deliberately contains those bare nouns).\n- Adopting a single dominant hazard type: rejected -- 7549 is an\n  explicitly broad/generic residual \"Not Elsewhere Classified\"\n  category with varied hand-tools and varied materials and no single\n  dominant hazard type per the task's CRITICAL guidance, so\n  `:hazard-type` stays a free-form field with generic examples\n  (hand-tool-hazard / material-handling-hazard / equipment-condition)\n  rather than a single hardcoded dominant hazard.\n- Merging `worker` and `workshop` into a single entity (only checking\n  one provenance record): rejected -- the two-entity provenance shape\n  (independently-registered worker AND independently-registered\n  workshop) matches the CRITICAL invariant that a \"worker/workshop\n  record must be independently verified/registered before any action\"\n  and the established pattern from prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actors\n  with two-sided provenance checks.\n- Reusing 7319's `:artisan-id` field name verbatim: rejected -- 7549's\n  title (\"Craft and Related Workers\", not \"Handicraft Workers\") is\n  broader than the artisan/handicraft framing, and the task's own\n  domain-design wording explicitly says \"worker/workshop record\", so\n  the field was renamed to `:worker-id` throughout for terminology\n  accuracy, with no change to the underlying provenance-check\n  structure.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-work-record`** -- job/commission/progress data logging\n   only. Requires an independently registered worker AND workshop\n   (`:no-worker`/`:no-workshop`). Not a craft-execution or\n   quality/safety-clearance decision in any respect -- it is a\n   records-management action.\n2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- crew/task scheduling proposal\n   only. Cannot carry a craft-execution decision (structurally absent\n   from the op's field shape; any such attempt via free text is\n   caught by the `:scope-excluded-action` content check). Does not\n   authorize, finalize, or draw any conclusion about a specific craft\n   item -- it is a calendar/logistics action.\n3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces a hand-tool-hazard/\n   material-handling/equipment-condition concern to a human/shop\n   safety officer; ALWAYS escalates, unconditionally, at every\n   confidence level, NEVER auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY\n   channel through which a safety observation may be surfaced, and it\n   explicitly never resolves, decides, or acts on anything itself --\n   the shop safety officer reviews and acts entirely on their own\n   authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- craft-materials procurement\n   proposal only (item, cost). Not a craft-execution or quality/\n   safety-clearance decision of any kind -- it is a\n   purchasing-logistics action, gated by ordinary cost-ceiling\n   escalation (above 2000 always escalates).\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\na craft-execution decision (e.g. deciding a craft item is finished), a\nproduct-quality/safety-clearance decision (e.g. declaring an item\nquality- or safety-cleared), or a shop-safety-officer-judgment\noverride -- these categories are structurally absent, not gated,\nconfirmed by: (a) the closed four-op allowlist itself\n(`craftnec.governor/allowed-ops`), (b) six concretely-named\nforbidden-op fixtures rejected at the governor-unit level\n(`craftnec.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`craftnec.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-craft-item`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-craft-execution-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-declare-product-quality-cleared`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-declare-safety-clearance`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-shop-safety-officer-judgment`),\nand (c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op`\nfield (`craftnec.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`,\n`.../contains-excluded-phrase?`), tested directly against a rationale\nthat attempts to finalize a craft item, declare a product\nquality-cleared, or override a shop safety officer's judgment even via\nan otherwise-allowlisted op\n(`craftnec.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-even-with-allowlisted-op`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-declare-quality-cleared`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-override-shop-safety-officer`).\n")))
